@page "/exchart/candles"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Chart

<DocsPage Product="ExChart" Title="Candles and intervals"
          Lead="A Price Range draws one candle a row, with its volume in a panel below, and refuses a row whose values disagree. An interval belongs to the series it qualifies and says what it means.">

    <DocsSection Title="Candles and volume">
        <p>
            <code>ChartSeries.Candles</code> takes one <code>PriceBar</code> a row: its open, high, low and close,
            and optionally its volume. A rising candle, its close above its own open, is hollow, and a falling one
            filled, so the two are told apart in print and without colour; a candle whose open equals its close is
            one line, and the tooltip says "unchanged". The tooltip gives the period, the four values and the change
            from the previous close. Volume is drawn as bars in a panel below that share the candles' axis, each
            following its candle's rule, and its axis starts at zero; the price axis does not.
        </p>
        <p>
            A row whose values disagree, a high below its open, is refused by name, never repaired. A row missing
            any of the four is a gap. A Price Range stands on a Dates axis only.
        </p>
        <p>
            The colours are Visual Tokens: <code>--ex-chart-rise</code> and <code>--ex-chart-fall</code>, by default
            both the series' own colour. Japan's red and blue or the West's green and red are the page's setting,
            as in the Example below. The 25-day average is the page's own calculation, an ordinary Level.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(Candles)" Mud="typeof(CandlesMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/IndexSessions.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Merged to fit, and narrowed back">
        <p>
            A hollow body needs about three device pixels to be told from a filled one. Past that, consecutive
            candles merge on calendar boundaries, into weeks, then months, quarters and years, the first that fits:
            the market's own weekly and monthly bars, the first open, the highest high, the lowest low, the last close,
            and the volume summed. The Notes say so ("Index: weekly candles, merged from rows to fit."), and the
            tooltip gives the period and how many rows it holds. A click on a merged candle narrows the range to
            its rows; a drag narrows to what it covers, and the rows come back as they are. A Consumer may fix the
            period with <code>MergeTo</code>; one that does not fit draws nothing and says to narrow the range.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Calendar time, or packed sessions">
        <p>
            By default candles stand on calendar time, as every other series does, so a weekend is a gap, and
            the gap is true. <code>ChartAxis.Dates(...) with { Packed = true }</code> gives each row one evenly spaced
            slot and leaves the closed days out; the ticks are still the real dates, and the Notes say the dates are
            packed. On a packed axis every series must stand on the candles' positions; one that does not is refused.
            The chart holds no View State, so the switch is the page's, as the checkbox above is.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Intervals">
        <p>
            An interval is not a series. It is a <code>SeriesInterval</code> on the series it qualifies, with the
            bounds the page hands in and a meaning that is required: <code>95% CI</code>, <code>±1σ</code>,
            <code>Bid–Ask</code>. The meaning is said in the legend, the tooltip
            (<code>3.32% [3.30%, 3.34%] Bid–Ask</code>), the Notes and the CSV's column names. ExChart computes
            no width, so an asymmetric interval is drawn as it is. A lower bound above its upper is refused; a point
            missing a bound has no interval there.
        </p>
        <p>
            On a Level an interval is drawn as a band or as whiskers, an appearance choice. An Observation takes
            whiskers both ways, <code>Interval</code> up and <code>XInterval</code> across, one bound a row in its
            points' order; where marks merge, the mark carries the widest of their intervals.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(Intervals)" Mud="typeof(IntervalsMud)" />
        <p>
            The <MudLink Href="showcase/equity-index" Target="_blank">Equity index daily bars showcase</MudLink>
            puts ten years of an index beside its grid, and these intervals in a second view.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
